"Harmonic functions with polynomial growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature (https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07534)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Title: Harmonic functions with polynomial growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature ... Suppose $(M,g)$ is a Riemannian manifold having dimension $n$, nonnegative Ricci curvature, maximal volume growth and unique tangent cone at infinity. ... In this paper, we prove a upper bound of $h_{k}$ in terms of the counting function of eigenvalues of $X$. ... These results are sharp, as they recover the corresponding well-known properties of $h_{k}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})$.\nTitle: Harmonic functions with polynomial growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature\nAuthors: Xian-Tao Huang\nDate: Wed Sep 15 19:02:26 2021\n\nSuppose $(M,g)$ is a Riemannian manifold having dimension $n$, nonnegative Ricci curvature, maximal volume growth and unique tangent cone at infinity. In this case, the tangent cone at infinity $C(X)$ is an Euclidean cone over the cross-section $X$. Denote by $\\alpha=\\lim_{r\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{\\mathrm{Vol}(B_{r}(p))}{r^{n}}$ the asymptotic volume ratio. Let $h_{k}=h_{k}(M)$ be the dimension of the space of harmonic functions with polynomial growth of growth order at most $k$. In this paper, we prove a upper bound of $h_{k}$ in terms of the counting function of eigenvalues of $X$. As a corollary, we obtain $\\lim_{k\\rightarrow\\infty}k^{1-n}h_{k}=\\frac{2\\alpha}{(n-1)!\\omega_{n}}$. These results are sharp, as they recover the corresponding well-known properties of $h_{k}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})$. In particular, these results hold on manifolds with nonnegative sectional curvature and maximal volume growth.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the asymptotic behavior of the dimension of spaces of harmonic functions with polynomial growth (https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.05008)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; Suppose $(M^{n},g)$ is a Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature, and let $h_{d}(M)$ be the dimension of the space of harmonic functions with polynomial growth of growth order at most $d$. ... We study the behavior of $h_{d}(M)$ when $d$ is large in this paper.More precisely, suppose that $(M^{n},g)$ has maximal volume growth and has a unique tangent cone at infinity, then when $d$ is sufficiently large, we obtain some estimates of $h_{d}(M)$ in terms of the growth order $d$, the dimension $n$ and the the asymptotic volume ratio $\\alpha=\\lim_{R\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{\\mathrm{Vol}(B_{p}(R))}{R^{n}}$.\nTitle: On the asymptotic behavior of the dimension of spaces of harmonic functions with polynomial growth\nAuthors: Xian-Tao Huang\nDate: Sun May 14 17:34:17 2017\n\nSuppose $(M^{n},g)$ is a Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature, and let $h_{d}(M)$ be the dimension of the space of harmonic functions with polynomial growth of growth order at most $d$. Colding and Minicozzi proved that $h_{d}(M)$ is finite. Later on, there are many researches which give better estimates of $h_{d}(M)$. We study the behavior of $h_{d}(M)$ when $d$ is large in this paper. More precisely, suppose that $(M^{n},g)$ has maximal volume growth and has a unique tangent cone at infinity, then when $d$ is sufficiently large, we obtain some estimates of $h_{d}(M)$ in terms of the growth order $d$, the dimension $n$ and the the asymptotic volume ratio $\\alpha=\\lim_{R\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{\\mathrm{Vol}(B_{p}(R))}{R^{n}}$. When $\\alpha=\\omega_{n}$, i.e., $(M^{n},g)$ is isometric to the Euclidean space, the asymptotic behavior obtained in this paper recovers a well-known asymptotic property of $h_{d}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDimension of polynomial growth harmonic functions on locally conformally flat manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04553)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Title: Dimension of polynomial growth harmonic functions on locally conformally flat manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature ... Date: Wed Aug  5 07:46:41 2026 ... The second question asks whether a sharp upper bound by its Euclidean analog $\\operatorname{dim}\\mathcal{H}_{d}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is true.\nTitle: Dimension of polynomial growth harmonic functions on locally conformally flat manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature\nAuthors: Xiaohan Cai, Mijia Lai\nDate: Wed Aug  5 07:46:41 2026\n\nLet $\\mathcal{H}_d(M)$ denote the space of harmonic functions of polynomial growth at most degree $d$ on a complete Riemannian manifold. Yau raised two fundamental questions regarding $\\mathcal{H}_d(M)$ on complete manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature. The first question is the finite dimensionality of $\\mathcal{H}_d(M)$, which is confirmed by Colding and Minicozzi. The second question asks whether a sharp upper bound by its Euclidean analog $\\operatorname{dim}\\mathcal{H}_{d}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is true. We verify that if $(M,g)$ is a locally conformally flat manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature, then the sharp Euclidean bound holds, i.e., $\\operatorname{dim}(\\mathcal{H}_{d}(M))\\leq \\operatorname{dim}(\\mathcal{H}_d(\\mathbb{R}^n))$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBiharmonic caloric functions on open manifolds - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926224526000501)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Volume 103, June 2026, 102379 ... For a complete open Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature, we show that the dimension of the space of ancient biharmonic caloric functions with polynomial growth is bounded by the degree of growth times the dimension of biharmonic functions with the same growth. ... In 1974, Yau [26] proved that any positive harmonic function on a complete noncompact Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature must be constant, which is known as the Liouville theorem for harmonic functions. ... In this paper we generalize Yau's conjecture to prove that the linear space of biharmonic functions of polynomial growth and ancient biharmonic caloric functions of polynomial growth are both finite-dimensional under the assumption that M is an open manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature. ... However, in order to get the Weyl type upper bound of ${\\mathcal{H}}_{d}^{2}(M)$ and ${\\mathcal{P}}_{d}^{2}(M)$, we need to apply the Weyl bound in the harmonic case (see [10], [11], [25]), which requires the nonnegative curvature condition.\n\n## Differential Geometry and its Applications\n\nVolume 103, June 2026, 102379\n\n# Biharmonic caloric functions on open manifolds\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.difgeo.2026.102379Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a complete open Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature, we show that the dimension of the space of ancient biharmonic caloric functions with polynomial growth is bounded by the degree of growth times the dimension of biharmonic functions with the same growth. Moreover, we investigate biharmonic caloric functions on Euclidean spaces.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn 1974, Yau [26] proved that any positive harmonic function on a complete noncompact Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature must be constant, which is known as the Liouville theorem for harmonic functions. Furthermore, he conjectured that the space of harmonic functions with polynomial growth must be finite-dimensional given that $\\mathrm{\\text{Ric}}\\ge 0$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHarmonic functions with polynomial growt... (https://axi.lims.ac.uk/paper/2109.07534)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Harmonic functions with polynomial growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature ... Suppose $(M,g)$ is a Riemannian manifold having dimension $n$, nonnegative Ricci curvature, maximal volume growth and unique tangent cone at infinity. ... In this paper, we prove a upper bound of $h_{k}$ in terms of the counting function of eigenvalues of $X$. ... These results are sharp, as they recover the corresponding well-known properties of $h_{k}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})$.\n\nID: 2109.07534\n\nSearch\n\n# Harmonic functions with polynomial growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature\n\nSeptember 15, 2021\n\nView on ArXiv\n\nXian-Tao Huang\n\nMathematics\n\nDifferential Geometry\n\nSuppose $(M,g)$ is a Riemannian manifold having dimension $n$, nonnegative Ricci curvature, maximal volume growth and unique tangent cone at infinity. In this case, the tangent cone at infinity $C(X)$ is an Euclidean cone over the cross-section $X$. Denote by $\\alpha=\\lim_{r\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{\\mathrm{Vol}(B_{r}(p))}{r^{n}}$ the asymptotic volume ratio. Let $h_{k}=h_{k}(M)$ be the dimension of the space of harmonic functions with polynomial growth of growth order at most $k$. In this paper, we prove a upper bound of $h_{k}$ in terms of the counting function of eigenvalues of $X$. As a corollary, we obtain $\\lim_{k\\rightarrow\\infty}k^{1-n}h_{k}=\\frac{2\\alpha}{(n-1)!\\omega_{n}}$. These results are sharp, as they recover the corresponding well-known properties of $h_{k}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})$. In particular, these results hold on manifolds with nonnegative sectional curvature and maximal volume growth.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe growth rate of harmonic functions (https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02627)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.8 years ago; We obtain the sharp gradient estimate of positive harmonic function in geodesic ball of complete surface with nonnegative curvature.On complete Riemannian manifolds with non-negative Ricci curvature and maximal volume growth, further assume the dimension of the manifold is not less than three, we prove that quantitative strong unique continuation yields the existence of nonconstant polynomial growth harmonic functions.\nTitle: The growth rate of harmonic functions\nAuthors: Guoyi Xu\nDate: Thu Dec  5 15:03:01 2019\n\nWe study the growth rate of harmonic functions in two aspects: gradient estimate and frequency. We obtain the sharp gradient estimate of positive harmonic function in geodesic ball of complete surface with nonnegative curvature. On complete Riemannian manifolds with non-negative Ricci curvature and maximal volume growth, further assume the dimension of the manifold is not less than three, we prove that quantitative strong unique continuation yields the existence of nonconstant polynomial growth harmonic functions. Also the uniform bound of frequency for linear growth harmonic functions on such manifolds is obtained, and this confirms a special case of Colding-Minicozzi conjecture on frequency.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Harmonic functions with polynomial growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354653359_Harmonic_functions_with_polynomial_growth_on_manifolds_with_nonnegative_Ricci_curvature?_tp=eyJjb250ZXh0Ijp7InBhZ2UiOiJzY2llbnRpZmljQ29udHJpYnV0aW9ucyIsInByZXZpb3VzUGFnZSI6bnVsbCwic3ViUGFnZSI6bnVsbH19)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; There are manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature on which any harmonic functionwith polynomial growth is constant, see Theorem 1 in [37] and Corollary 8.12 of [16]. ... vature, maximal volume growth and has a unique tangent cone at inﬁnity, then h k(M)≥ ... In the following, we state the lower bound theorem in the special case that the ... 6 XIAN-TAO HUANG ... The methods in this paper can be applied to prove similar dimension esti-\n\ngrowth, some additional assumptions on the manifold are necessary.\n\nIn Theorem 0.1 of [19], Ding proved that suppose (M n, g) has nonnegative Ricci cur-\n\nvature, maximal volume growth and has a unique tangent cone at inﬁnity, then h k(M)≥\n\nCk n−1 for some constant C depending on n and α. Note that the power n−1 in this result\n\nis sharp compared to the Euclidean case. In a recent paper, Xu obtained the existence of\n\nnontrivial harmonic functions with polynomial growth on some manifolds satisfying cer-\n\ntain assumptions on its tangent cone at inﬁnity, see Theorem 1.7 in [40]. Note that Xu’s\n\nexistence theorem does not require the manifold to have maximal volume growth, and\n\nan explicit example of a collapsed manifold satisfying the assumptions in Xu’s existence\n\ntheorem was constructed in [40].\n\nIn [19] and [40], the ideas to ﬁnd nontrivial harmonic functions with polynomial growth\n\nare similar. Making use of the conic structure at inﬁnity, we transplant suitable harmonic\n\nfunctions on the tangent cone at inﬁnity back to obtain harmonic functions deﬁned on\n\nlarger and larger geodesic balls of the manifold. By construction and making use of certain\n\nthree circles theorems, we can prove these harmonic functions satisfy the desired growth\n\nrate. Finally by Arzela-Ascoli theorem we construct a harmonic function with the desired\n\ngrowth rate on the whole manifold. In [40], Xu proved a three circles theorem, which\n\nis diﬀerent from the one given by Ding, see Theorem 3.2 in [40] and Lemma 1.1 in [19]\n\nrespectively.\n\nLater on, following the ideas of Xu, the author obtained an improved lower bound of\n\nh k(M) when M satisﬁes the same assumptions as in Xu’s existence theorem, see Theorem\n\n1.2 in [26]. This result recovers Theorem 0.1 in [19] if the manifold has maximal volume\n\ngrowth. In the following, we state the lower bound theorem in the special case that the\n\nmanifold has maximal volume growth, the more general cases can consult Theorem 1.2 in\n\n[26].\n\nTheorem 1.5 (See [19] and [26]).Suppose (M n, g)is a complete Riemannian manifold\n\nwith nonnegative Ricci curvature with maximal volume growth. Assume M has a unique\n\ntangent cone at inﬁnity, denoted by (C(X), d C(X), m C(X)), which is an Euclidean cone over\n\nthe cross section (X, d X, m X). Let 0 = λ 0< λ 1≤λ 2≤... be the eigenvalues (counted\n\nwith multiplicity) for the Laplacian operator on (X, d X, m X). Let N X:R+→Z+be the\n\ncounting function with respect to (X, d X, m X), i.e.\n\nN X(λ) := #{i∈Z+∪ {0}|λ i≤λ}.(1.6)\n\nThen given any k > 0 such that k(k+n−2) > λ 1,\n\nh k≥N X(β(β+n−2))(1.7)\n\nholds for any positive number β < k.\n\n4 XIAN-TAO HUANG\n\nThe key point in the proof of Theorem 1.5 is based on the method in [40]: we use\n\nL 2-orthonormal eigenfunctions of the cross section X to construct as many as possible\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe growth rate of harmonic functions - Xu - 2020 - Journal of the London Mathematical Society - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1112/jlms.12306)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; We obtain the sharp gradient estimate of positive harmonic function in geodesic ball of complete surface with nonnegative curvature.On complete Riemannian manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature and maximal volume growth, further assume the dimension of the manifold is not less than 3, we prove that quantitative strong unique continuation yields the existence of nonconstant polynomial growth harmonic functions.Also the uniform bound of frequency for linear growth harmonic functions on such manifolds is obtained, and this confirms a special case of Colding–Minicozzi's conjecture on frequency. ... 20 (2016) 2905–2922.\n\nVolume 101, Issue 3 pp. 1298-1319\n\nResearch Article\n\n# The growth rate of harmonic functions\n\nGuoyi Xu, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nGuoyi Xu\n\n  * guoyixu@tsinghua.edu.cn\n\nDepartment of Mathematical Sciences, Tsinghua University, Beijing, 100084 P. R. China\n\nguoyixu@tsinghua.edu.cn\n\nFirst published: 03 February 2020\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1112/jlms.12306Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\nThe author was partially supported by NSFC-11771230, Beijing Natural Science Foundation Z190003.\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe study the growth rate of harmonic functions in two aspects: gradient estimate and frequency. We obtain the sharp gradient estimate of positive harmonic function in geodesic ball of complete surface with nonnegative curvature. On complete Riemannian manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature and maximal volume growth, further assume the dimension of the manifold is not less than 3, we prove that quantitative strong unique continuation yields the existence of nonconstant polynomial growth harmonic functions. Also the uniform bound of frequency for linear growth harmonic functions on such manifolds is obtained, and this confirms a special case of Colding–Minicozzi's conjecture on frequency.\n\nReferences\n\n  * 1 A. Aché, D. Maximo and H. Wu, ‘Metrics with nonnegative Ricci curvature on convex three-manifolds’, Geom. Topol. 20 (2016) 2905–2922.\n\nWeb of Science® Google Scholar\n\n  * 2 F. J. Almgren, Jr., ‘ Dirichlet's problem for multiple valued functions and the regularity of mass minimizing integral currents’, Minimal submanifolds and geodesics (ed. M. Obata; North-Holland, Amsterdam, 1979) 1–6.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  * 3 S. Axler, P. Bourdon and W. Ramey, Harmonic function theory, 2nd edn, Graduate Texts in Mathematics 137 (Springer, New York, NY, 2001).\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  * 4 J. Cheeger and T. H. Colding, ‘Lower bounds on Ricci curvature and the almost rigidity of warped products’, Ann. of Math. (2) 144 (1996) 189–237.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmath/preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/interio